{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"synonymous-destined-cricket","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Observation\n`ensure_fresh` (`src/rebar/_engine_support/reads.py:192`) spawns `git rev-parse --verify <branch>` (line 221) on every read, before it checks the 60 s `/tmp/.ticket-sync-*` marker throttle. A throttled read therefore still costs 1 git process. Measured on the `rebar audit serve` index (`src/rebar/audit/server.py` `_audited_tickets`): one render with 1 audited ticket spawns 5 git processes while throttled (1 from `list_tickets`, 4 from the `show_ticket` / `verify_signature` reads inside `audit_trail`), so the index costs 1 + 4N git spawns per request for N tickets. Under host load that per-spawn cost dominates the request.\n\nDiscovered while root-causing smalltime-repressive-sealion (b0f0-3dd2-fcf2-4707).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A throttled read (marker younger than 60 s) spawns no git process, shown by a test that counts git spawns.\n- [ ] The branch-existence check still runs before any fetch on an unthrottled read.","id":"40c3-d22a-3872-4c8c","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Throttled reads still spawn a git rev-parse each"},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793804211554605001,"uuid":"67d87b1e-1ed6-428c-a217-913c82b75723"}